    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Harshavardhana belonged to the Pushyabhuti (Vardhana) dynasty and unified Northern India after the fall of the Imperial Guptas, shifting his capital from Thanesar to Kannauj.",
        "Banabhatta, Harsha's court poet, authored the famous biography 'Harshacharita' and the Sanskrit prose romance 'Kadambari'.",
        "Harsha was himself a literary scholar who wrote three renowned Sanskrit plays: 'Ratnavali', 'Priyadarsika', and 'Nagananda'.",
        "The Chinese Buddhist monk Hiuen Tsang (Xuanzang) visited India during Harsha's reign, attended the Kannauj Assembly, and studied at Nalanda University under Silabhadra.",
        "Harsha regularly organized the quinquennial religious assembly known as 'Mahamoksha Parishad' at Prayag to distribute royal wealth among monks, scholars, and the needy."
      ],
      "id": "s5vtcyf"
